      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fort Greene Ville, built in November 1793 by General Anthony Wayne's Legion, hosted the Treaty of Greenville signing on August 3, 1795.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenville%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The fort’s wooden defenses spanned about 55 acres, making it North America’s largest wooden fort.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenville%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the war, the Army abandoned Fort Greenville in 1796; it was partly burned later that year to recover construction nails.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenville%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bear’s Mill, constructed in 1849, remains a stone grinding flour mill that still produces cornmeal, whole‑wheat flour, rye flour, and pancake mixes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
